To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = Σ(n * ΔS_f) - Σ(n * ΔS_f)

where n is the number of moles of the substance and ΔS_f is the change in entropy of formation.

The reaction is:

3CO(g) + O3(g) → 3CO2(g)

We need the standard Gibbs free energy of formation (ΔS_f) for each reactant and product at 298 K:

ΔS_f(CO,g) = 23.1 J/mol·K
ΔS_f(O3,g) = 23.1 J/mol·K
ΔS_f(CO2,g) = 20.8 J/mol·K

Now we can calculate the change in entropy (ΔS):

ΔS = (3 * ΔS_f(CO2,g)) - (3 * ΔS_f(CO,g) + 3 * ΔS_f(O3,g))

ΔS = (3 * 20.8 J/mol·K) - (3 * 23.1 J/mol·K + 3 * 23.1 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 62.4 J/K - (69.3 J/mol·K + 69.3 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 62.4 J/K - 138.6 J/K

ΔS = -76.2 J/K

The change in entropy for the reaction is -76.2 J/K.
