","The selectivity factor \( s = k_e / k_m \) is a measure of the stere-to-linear reaction ratio in a dimerization process. It quantifies the ratio of the rate of homocleftomeric dimer formation to the rate of meso dimer formation. The higher the \( s \) value, the faster the homocomomeric dimer forms compared to the meso dimer. This factor is directly related to the enhancement of the enantiomeric efficiency (e.e.) of the original polarized species.

The relationship between \( s \) and the enhancement of \( e.e \) is given by the formula:

\[
e.e_{\text{enhanced}} = \frac{s - 1}{s + 1} \cdot e.E_{\text{initial}}
\]

