","To find the distance where B crosses A, we first determine the distance A travels in the first 3 days.  
Distance travelled by A in 3 hours:  
\[ \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ km/h} \times 3 \text{ h} = 30 \text{ km} \]  
When B crosses A, they will travel the same distance from the starting point. Let \( t \) be the time (in hours) that B travels to catch A.  
Distance covered by A:  
A travels at 10 km/hr, and the remaining time is \( t \), so the distance covered by A is \( 30 + 10t \).  
Distance covered by B:  
B travels at 20 km/hr, so the distance covered by B is \( 20t \).  
Equating the distances:  
\[ 30 + 10t = 20t \]  
\[ 30 = 10t \]  
\[ t = 3 \text{ hours} \]  
Distance from the starting point when B catches A:  
\[ \text{Distance} = 20 \times 3 = 60 \text{ km} \]  
Thus, B catches A at a distance of \(\boxed{60 \text{ km}}\) from the starting point.",fluency,"# Task Description
