A triangular-shaped cantilever beam of uniform thickness \( t \), width \( b \) at the fixed end, and length \( L \) is subjected to a concentrated load \( P \) at the free end. The Young’s modulus of the material is \( E \). What is the maximum deflection of the beam?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The maximum deflection of a triangular-canted beam under a concentrated load \( P \) at the free end is given by:
\[
\delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{2Pt^3}{3EI}
\]
where:
- \( \delta_{\text{max}} \) is the maximum deflection,
- \( P \) is the concentrated load,
- \( t \) is the thickness of the beam,
- \( L \) is the length of the beam,
- \( E \) is the Young's modulus of the material,
- \( b \) is the width of the beam at the fixed end.

The moment of inertia \( I \) for a trapezoidal beam is:
\[
I = \frac{1}{3} \left( \frac{b + b + t}{3} \right) t^3 = \frac{1}{3} \left( \frac{2b + t}{3} \right) t^3 = \frac{(2b + t)t^3}{9}
\]
Substituting \( I \) into the deflection formula:
\[
\delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{2Pt^3}{3E \left( \frac{(2b + t)t^3}{9} \right)} = \frac{2Pt^3}{3E \left( \frac{(2b + t)t^3}{9} \right)} = \frac{6Pt}{3E \left( 2b + t \right)} = \frac{2Pt}{E (2b + t)}
\]
Thus, the maximum deflection is:
\[
\boxed{\delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{2Pt}{E (2b + t)}}
\]",relevance,"# Task Description
