The entropy production rate \( \pi_S \) is given by:
\[
\pi_S = \frac{1}{T} \dot{S} = \frac{1}{T} \dot{S}_{\text{rev}} + \frac{1}{T} \dot{S}_{\text{ir}}
\]
For a dissipative process like friction, the entropy production rate is:
\[
\pi_S = \frac{1}{T} \dot{S}_{\text{ir}} = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T}
\]
This is because the entropy production rate is equal to the heat generated by friction divided by the temperature. The friction force \( F_{\text{fr}} \) does work \( F_{\text{fr}} v \) over time, which generates heat as heat is lost to the environment.

Thus, the entropy production rate is:
\[
\boxed{\pi_S = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T}}
\]
