<USER_REQUEST>
An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \):**
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{100}{1.1} \approx 90.0 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

2. **Calculate the new voltage gain \( A_{vf'} \):**
   \[
   \text{Reduction in } A_v = 6 \text{ dB} \implies \text{New } A_{vf'} = 100 - 6 = 94 \text{ dB dB}
   \]
   \[
   A_{vf}' = \frac{A_{vf'}}{1 + m_v} = \frac{94}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{94}{1.1} \approx 85.1 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

3. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \):**
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf'} - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{85.1 - 90.0}{90.0} \right) \times 100 \approx -10.7\%
   \]

   **Final Answer:** The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{-10.7\%}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

