","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m}
\]  
The force on the proton is given by:  
\[
F = qE
\]  
Substituting \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\):  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19}) \times (500) = 800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}
\]  
Now, substitute \(F = 800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}\) and \(m = 1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\):  
\[
a = \frac{800 \times 10^{-19}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27}} = 4.81 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.81 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The acceleration is constant, so we use the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = u + at
\]  
Given \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), \(u = 0 \, \text{m/s}\) (since the proton starts from rest), and \(a = 4.81 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2\):  
\[
35,000 = 0 + (4.81 \times 10^8) t
\]  
Solve for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{35,000}{4.81 \times 10^8} = 7.31 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}
\]  
